Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:49:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 16/24] MS2000: memory-appetite hunt instruments - the recreate
 ping-pong at P:0xFF0000 named

MEASURED (this round, warden-guarded runs, stderr-unbuffered instruments):
- [JIT-CHAIN] one-time print per chain: pSize=0x1000000, sizeof(JitCacheEntry)=16 B,
  MMU active on Windows for both tables -> per-chain table ceiling is 384 MB. The 4+ GB
  appetite is NOT the tables.
- [JIT-TRAMP] dispatch window baked = 0x1000000 (correct, never fires at boot).
- ZERO "outside the compiled program window" hits -> the bounds checks are inert at boot.
- ZERO notifyPMemWrite hits in the bootstrap area (>= 0xFF0000) -> P-writes are not the destroyer.
- [JIT] funcRecreate #N pc=0xFF0000 - an ETERNAL recreate churn at the vector/bootstrap entry,
  ~320/s, every sampled pc identical.
- [JIT] makeRoom: create @ 0xFF0000 destroys block @ 0xFF0000 - the create's makeRoom destroys
  the block that already starts at the requested pc (occupancy at _pc+1 within the first
  instruction). Mechanism (see occupyArea): interior words of every block carry &funcRecreate;
  a mid-block dispatch destroys+re-emits, freeing the previous start, which is then re-created,
  rotating forever. Each cycle feeds the asmjit pool (which never shrinks) -> 4+ GB commit,
  warden kill at 4296 MB. Same pool-churn class as the Event 1003 system OOM during the
  ORIGINAL code run (06:29:46) - the appetite predates the nesting fix.

INSTRUMENTS (all rate-limited, stderr, survive abort):
- JitBlockChain ctor: chain table anatomy print.
- funcRecreate: total counter + every-4096th pc sample.
- makeRoomForFirstInstruction: every-1024th destroy pair (create-pc -> destroyed-pc).
- notifyPMemWrite: every-1024th write into the bootstrap area (>= 0xFF0000).
- Jit::getChainCount/getCurrentBlockCount/getCurrentCodeSize/getRecreateCount accessors;
  live-block counter (emit ++ / release --) in JitBlockChain.
- Engine per-second status line moved to stderr (survives process death) + chains/blocks/
  codeMB/recreates columns.

NEXT (open): the fix is block-linking for mid-block entries (jump into the existing block's
machine code at the right offset instead of destroy+re-emit), or a single-op fast path for
the vector slots. Design decision pending Thor.
